use std
into form_sdf.amni
dialect js

This file is a library built for the web as a wasm module; its `main()` does nothing. JavaScript drives it through its exported functions (every name starting with fs_ is exported). It is a port of the evaluation and meshing core of cad/form_sdf.js: the signed distance of every primitive shape, the shape modifiers (rotation, taper, twist and bend deformers, noise, shell, onion, offset, array and mirror copies), the smooth boolean blend with colors, the sculpt brushes, the surface-nets mesher with normals, projection and colors, and the per-vertex skin weights. JavaScript compiles the text and stores every shape and brush as numbers in the arrays described below. Results are compared bit for bit with the JavaScript, so every floating-point formula keeps the JavaScript evaluation order exactly: each sum starts at 0.0 and adds its terms one at a time in the order written, products and quotients group left to right as written, a negation is a real negation (never 0.0 minus a value), and nothing is fused, reordered or vectorized. Reals are f64.

```amni
extern fn fs_view_f64(id i32, xs real[:]) = "fs_view_f64";
extern fn fs_view_f32(id i32, xs f32[:]) = "fs_view_f32";
extern fn fs_view_i32(id i32, xs i32[:]) = "fs_view_i32";
extern fn fs_view_u8(id i32, xs u8[:]) = "fs_view_u8";
extern fn fs_cos(x real) real = "fs_cos";
extern fn fs_sin(x real) real = "fs_sin";
extern fn fs_atan2(y real, x real) real = "fs_atan2";
extern fn fs_exp(x real) real = "fs_exp";
extern fn fs_pow(x real, y real) real = "fs_pow";
extern fn fs_mod(x real, y real) real = "fs_mod";
```

Module state. Ints, all starting at 0: `NS`, `NB`, `NW`, `NA`, `NVX`, `EVC`. Reals, all starting at 0.0: `CK`, `ST`, `WX`, `WY`, `WZ`. Growable arrays: f64 `PF`, `BS`, `DC`, `CO`, `QQ`, `PR`, `KK`, `DP`, `AC`, `CV8`; f32 `V`, `POS`, `NRM`, `COL`, `WT`, `XF`; i32 `SHI`, `BRI`, `LW`, `LA`, `CV`, `IDX`, `KP`, `JN`, `TOP`, `XI`; u8 `DN`, `MAT`. `GR` is the constant list of 36 reals 1.0, 1.0, 0.0, −1.0, 1.0, 0.0, 1.0, −1.0, 0.0, −1.0, −1.0, 0.0, 1.0, 0.0, 1.0, −1.0, 0.0, 1.0, 1.0, 0.0, −1.0, −1.0, 0.0, −1.0, 0.0, 1.0, 1.0, 0.0, −1.0, 1.0, 0.0, 1.0, −1.0, 0.0, −1.0, −1.0. `EDG` is the constant list of 24 ints 0, 1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6, 7, 0, 2, 1, 3, 4, 6, 5, 7, 0, 4, 1, 5, 2, 6, 3, 7 (cube edge e joins corners `EDG`[2e] and `EDG`[2e+1]; corner c sits at offsets c and 1, (c shifted right 1) and 1, c shifted right 2). Every push onto `POS` is done with puf and every push onto `IDX` with pui, never with a bare push.

Growth helpers. `XF` and `XI` are scratch arrays used only by these helpers. `cwf(xs, m)` takes an f32 growable array by reference and makes xs.cap at least m without changing its entries: when m is above xs.cap, `XF` is emptied and then gets `reserve`(the length of xs), then gets a copy of xs with one `append`; xs is emptied and then gets `reserve`(m), then gets `XF` back with one `append`; `XF` is emptied. `cwi(xs, m)` is the same for an i32 growable array with `XI` as the scratch. `puf(xs, x real)` takes an f32 growable array by reference: when the length of xs is at least xs.cap, cwf(xs, 2·(the length of xs) plus 64); then x as f32 is pushed onto xs. `pui(xs, x int)` is the same for an i32 growable array with cwi, pushing x as i32.

`jmin(a real, b real) real` is JavaScript's Math.min of two numbers: NaN when a or b is NaN; otherwise when a equals b it is a when a is negative zero (1.0 divided by a is below 0) and b otherwise; otherwise the smaller of a and b. `jmax(a real, b real) real` is JavaScript's Math.max of two numbers: NaN when a or b is NaN; otherwise when a equals b it is b when a is negative zero and a otherwise; otherwise the larger of a and b. `hyp3(a real, b real, c real) real` is exactly V8's Math.hypot of three numbers: mx starts at 0.0; for each of a, b and c in order that is not NaN, its absolute value replaces mx when it is above mx; it returns infinity when mx equals infinity, NaN when any of a, b and c is NaN, 0.0 when mx equals 0; otherwise sum and comp start at 0.0 and, for v equal to the absolute values of a, b and c in that order, nv is v divided by mx, su is nv·nv minus comp, pre is sum plus su, comp becomes (pre minus sum) minus su and sum becomes pre; it returns sqrt(sum)·mx. `hyp2(a real, b real) real` is the same algorithm with only the two values a and b. `jsign(x real) real` is JavaScript's Math.sign: 1.0 when x is above 0, −1.0 when x is below 0, otherwise x itself. `cl(v real, a real, b real) real` is a when v is below a, otherwise b when v is above b, otherwise v. `len3(x real, y real, z real) real` is sqrt((x·x plus y·y) plus z·z). `bad(x real) bool` is true when x equals 0 or x is NaN (JavaScript's falsy number). `sm(u real) real` is (u·u)·(3.0 minus 2.0·u).

`i32of(v real) int` is JavaScript's ToInt32: 0 when v is NaN or its absolute value is infinity; otherwise n is v truncated toward zero as an int, and the result is n and 4294967295, minus 4294967296 when that is at least 2147483648. `imul(a int, b int) int` is JavaScript's Math.imul of two 32-bit ints: p is (a·b) and 4294967295, and the result is p minus 4294967296 when p is at least 2147483648, else p. `fade(t real) real` is ((t·t)·t)·((t·((t·6.0) minus 15.0)) plus 10.0).

`gn(x real, y real, z real) real` is JavaScript's gradient noise: X, Y and Z are floor(x), floor(y) and floor(z) (reals); fx is x minus X, fy is y minus Y, fz is z minus Z; u is fade(fx), v is fade(fy), w is fade(fz); the six corner hashes are computed once before the loop: hx0 is i32of(X), hx1 is i32of(X plus 1.0), hy0 is imul(i32of(Y), 374761393), hy1 is imul(i32of(Y plus 1.0), 374761393), hz0 is imul(i32of(Z), 668265261), hz1 is imul(i32of(Z plus 1.0), 668265261); r starts at 0.0. For c from 0 below 8 in order: i is c and 1, j is (c shifted right 1) and 1, k is c shifted right 2 (ints); hx is hx1 when i is 1 else hx0; hy is hy1 when j is 1 else hy0; hz is hz1 when k is 1 else hz0; h is imul((hx xor hy) xor hz, −1640531535); hu is (h and 4294967295) as u32; g is (((hu xor (hu shifted right 15)) mod 12) as an int)·3 (the mod is done on the u32); wa is u when i is 1 else 1.0 minus u; wb is v when j is 1 else 1.0 minus v; wc is w when k is 1 else 1.0 minus w; r becomes r plus ((wa·wb)·wc)·((`GR`[g]·(fx minus i as a real) plus `GR`[g+1]·(fy minus j as a real)) plus `GR`[g+2]·(fz minus k as a real)). It returns r. `fbm(x real, y real, z real) real` is ((gn(x, y, z) plus 0.5·gn(2.0·x plus 5.2, 2.0·y plus 1.3, 2.0·z plus 7.1)) plus 0.25·gn(4.0·x plus 2.9, 4.0·y plus 8.4, 4.0·z plus 3.7))·0.8.

`segd(px real, py real, pz real, oa int, ob int) real` is the distance from a point to a segment whose ends a and b are stored at `PF`[oa], `PF`[oa+1], `PF`[oa+2] and `PF`[ob], `PF`[ob+1], `PF`[ob+2]: bx is `PF`[ob] minus `PF`[oa], by is `PF`[ob+1] minus `PF`[oa+1], bz is `PF`[ob+2] minus `PF`[oa+2]; qx is px minus `PF`[oa], qy is py minus `PF`[oa+1], qz is pz minus `PF`[oa+2]; bb is (bx·bx plus by·by) plus bz·bz, replaced by 1e-18 when bad(bb); t is cl(((qx·bx plus qy·by) plus qz·bz) divided by bb, 0.0, 1.0); `ST` becomes t; it returns len3(qx minus bx·t, qy minus by·t, qz minus bz·t).

`poly(o int, n int, px real, py real) real` is the signed distance to a closed 2D polygon of n edges stored as 6 reals each from `PF`[o] (JavaScript precomputes them): edge i at e equal to o plus 6·i holds ax at e, ay at e+1, ex at e+2, ey at e+3, den at e+4 and by at e+5. d starts at 1e18 and s at 1.0; for i from 0 below n in order: wx is px minus ax, wy is py minus ay; t is cl((wx·ex plus wy·ey) divided by den, 0.0, 1.0); qx is wx minus ex·t, qy is wy minus ey·t; q is qx·qx plus qy·qy; when q is below d, d becomes q; c1 is py at least ay; c2 is py below by; when c1 equals c2 and c2 equals (ex·wy above ey·wx), s becomes the negation of s. It returns s·sqrt(d).

`ex2(d2 real, t real, hh real, b real) real`: wx is d2 plus b; wy is (abs(t) minus hh) plus b; it returns (jmin(jmax(wx, wy), 0.0) plus hyp2(jmax(wx, 0.0), jmax(wy, 0.0))) minus b.

`ngon(p int, px real, py real) real` is the 2D regular polygon distance with an equal to `PF`[p+16], rca equal to `PF`[p+14] and rsa equal to `PF`[p+15]: L is hyp2(px, py); tw is 2.0·an; b is fs_mod(fs_mod(fs_atan2(px, py), tw) plus tw, tw) minus an; xx is L·fs_cos(b) minus rca; yy is L·abs(fs_sin(b)) minus rsa; yy becomes yy plus cl(the negation of yy, 0.0, rsa); it returns hyp2(xx, yy)·jsign(xx).

`tre(e int, p0 real, p1 real, p2 real) real` with E0, E1, E2 equal to `PF`[e], `PF`[e+1], `PF`[e+2]: tt is cl(((E0·p0 plus E1·p1) plus E2·p2) divided by ((E0·E0 plus E1·E1) plus E2·E2), 0.0, 1.0); a0 is E0·tt minus p0, a1 is E1·tt minus p1, a2 is E2·tt minus p2; it returns (a0·a0 plus a1·a1) plus a2·a2. `tri(t int, x real, y real, z real) real` is the squared distance to the triangle whose 31 reals start at `PF`[t]: corners a at t, b at t+3, c at t+6, edges ba at t+9, cb at t+12, ac at t+15, normal nor at t+18, q1 at t+21, q2 at t+24, q3 at t+27, and nn at t+30 (each triple is three consecutive reals). pa is (x minus `PF`[t], y minus `PF`[t+1], z minus `PF`[t+2]), pb is (x minus `PF`[t+3], y minus `PF`[t+4], z minus `PF`[t+5]), pc is (x minus `PF`[t+6], y minus `PF`[t+7], z minus `PF`[t+8]); the dot of a stored triple at o with a local triple w is (`PF`[o]·w0 plus `PF`[o+1]·w1) plus `PF`[o+2]·w2. When (jsign(dot of q1 with pa) plus jsign(dot of q2 with pb)) plus jsign(dot of q3 with pc) is below 2, it returns jmin(jmin(tre(t+9, pa0, pa1, pa2), tre(t+12, pb0, pb1, pb2)), tre(t+15, pc0, pc1, pc2)); otherwise s is the dot of nor with pa and it returns (s·s) divided by `PF`[t+30].

Primitive distances. Shape s stores 8 ints at `SHI`[8s] to `SHI`[8s+7]: kind, op, flags, h (its header offset in `PF`), nT (copies), nd (deformer ops), mi (material index) and p (its primitive data offset in `PF`). `prim(s int, x real, y real, z real) real` reads kind and p of shape s and returns the distance of that kind, where c0, c1, c2 mean `PF`[p], `PF`[p+1], `PF`[p+2]:
kind 0 (sphere): len3(x minus c0, y minus c1, z minus c2) minus `PF`[p+3].
kind 1 (ellipsoid): X is x minus c0, Y is y minus c1, Z is z minus c2; rx, ry, rz are `PF`[p+3], `PF`[p+4], `PF`[p+5]; k0 is len3(X divided by rx, Y divided by ry, Z divided by rz); k1 is len3(X divided by (rx·rx), Y divided by (ry·ry), Z divided by (rz·rz)); the negation of jmin(jmin(rx, ry), rz) when k1 is below 1e-12, otherwise (k0·(k0 minus 1.0)) divided by k1.
kind 2 (box): rr is `PF`[p+6]; qx is (abs(x minus c0) minus `PF`[p+3]) plus rr, qy is (abs(y minus c1) minus `PF`[p+4]) plus rr, qz is (abs(z minus c2) minus `PF`[p+5]) plus rr; (len3(jmax(qx, 0.0), jmax(qy, 0.0), jmax(qz, 0.0)) plus jmin(jmax(jmax(qx, qy), qz), 0.0)) minus rr.
kind 3 (capsule): d is segd(x, y, z, p, p plus 3) and t is `ST` right after it; d minus (`PF`[p+6] plus (`PF`[p+7] minus `PF`[p+6])·t).
kind 4 (torus): q is hyp2(x minus c0, y minus c1) minus `PF`[p+3]; hyp2(q, z minus c2) minus `PF`[p+4].
kind 5 (cylinder): r is `PF`[p+3], bx, by, bz are `PF`[p+4], `PF`[p+5], `PF`[p+6], baba is `PF`[p+7]; px is x minus c0, py is y minus c1, pz is z minus c2; paba is (px·bx plus py·by) plus pz·bz; X is len3(px·baba minus bx·paba, py·baba minus by·paba, pz·baba minus bz·paba) minus r·baba; Y is abs(paba minus baba·0.5) minus baba·0.5; x2 is X·X; y2 is (Y·Y)·baba; d is the negation of jmin(x2, y2) when jmax(X, Y) is below 0, otherwise (x2 when X is above 0, else 0.0) plus (y2 when Y is above 0, else 0.0); (jsign(d)·sqrt(abs(d))) divided by baba.
kind 6 (cone): ra, rb, rba are `PF`[p+3], `PF`[p+4], `PF`[p+5]; bx, by, bz are `PF`[p+6], `PF`[p+7], `PF`[p+8]; baba is `PF`[p+9]; kk is `PF`[p+10]; px is x minus c0, py is y minus c1, pz is z minus c2; paba is ((px·bx plus py·by) plus pz·bz) divided by baba; X is sqrt(jmax(((px·px plus py·py) plus pz·pz) minus (paba·paba)·baba, 0.0)); cax is jmax(0.0, X minus (ra when paba is below 0.5, else rb)); cay is abs(paba minus 0.5) minus 0.5; ff is cl((rba·(X minus ra) plus paba·baba) divided by kk, 0.0, 1.0); cbx is (X minus ra) minus ff·rba; cby is paba minus ff; (−1.0 when cbx is below 0 and cay is below 0, else 1.0)·sqrt(jmin(cax·cax plus (cay·cay)·baba, cbx·cbx plus (cby·cby)·baba)).
kind 7 (prism): X is x minus c0, Y is y minus c1, Z is z minus c2; g is ngon(p, (X·`PF`[p+3] plus Y·`PF`[p+4]) plus Z·`PF`[p+5], (X·`PF`[p+6] plus Y·`PF`[p+7]) plus Z·`PF`[p+8]); ex2(g, (X·`PF`[p+9] plus Y·`PF`[p+10]) plus Z·`PF`[p+11], `PF`[p+12], `PF`[p+13]).
kind 8 (pyramid): hx, hy, H, lx, ly are `PF`[p+3] to `PF`[p+7]; X is abs(x minus c0), Y is abs(y minus c1), Z is z minus c2; m is jmax(jmax(the negation of Z, ((X·H plus Z·hx) minus hx·H) divided by lx), ((Y·H plus Z·hy) minus hy·H) divided by ly); m when m is at most 0; otherwise ex is jmax(X minus hx, 0.0), ey is jmax(Y minus hy, 0.0), and the result is sqrt(jmin(jmin(tri(p plus 8, X, Y, Z), tri(p plus 39, X, Y, Z)), (ex·ex plus ey·ey) plus Z·Z)).
kind 9 (wedge): ex2(poly(p plus 8, 3, y minus c1, z minus c2), x minus c0, `PF`[p+3], `PF`[p+6]).
kind 10 (tube): n is `PF`[p] as an int; g is p plus 1; br is g plus 12·n; m starts at 1e9; for i from 0 below n: o is g plus 12·i; skip this segment when len3(x minus `PF`[o+9], y minus `PF`[o+10], z minus `PF`[o+11]) minus `PF`[br+i] is at least m; qx is x minus `PF`[o], qy is y minus `PF`[o+1], qz is z minus `PF`[o+2]; t is cl(((qx·`PF`[o+3] plus qy·`PF`[o+4]) plus qz·`PF`[o+5])·`PF`[o+6], 0.0, 1.0); d is (len3(qx minus `PF`[o+3]·t, qy minus `PF`[o+4]·t, qz minus `PF`[o+5]·t) minus `PF`[o+7]) minus `PF`[o+8]·t; when d is below m, m becomes d. The result is m.
kind 11 (lathe): an is `PF`[p+3]; n is `PF`[p+4] as an int; when an is not 0: X is x minus c0, Y is y minus c1, tw is 2.0·an, b is fs_mod(fs_mod(fs_atan2(X, Y), tw) plus tw, tw) minus an, and the result is poly(p plus 5, n, hyp2(X, Y)·fs_cos(b), z minus c2); otherwise poly(p plus 5, n, hyp2(x minus c0, y minus c1), z minus c2).
kind 12 (extrude): n is `PF`[p+5] as an int; ex2(poly(p plus 6, n, x minus c0, y minus c1), z minus c2, `PF`[p+3], `PF`[p+4]).

`dinv(o int)` applies the inverse of the deformer op stored at `PF`[o] to the three entries of `QQ`. ty is `PF`[o] as an int and a is `PF`[o+1] as an int. When ty is 0 (taper): f is `PF`[o+2]; i0 is `PF`[o+3] as an int, i1 is `PF`[o+4] as an int; m0 is `PF`[o+5], m1 is `PF`[o+6], z0 is `PF`[o+7], L is `PF`[o+8]; sc is 1.0 plus (f minus 1.0)·cl((`QQ`[a] minus z0) divided by L, 0.0, 1.0); k is 1.0 divided by sc; `QQ`[i0] becomes m0 plus (`QQ`[i0] minus m0)·k, then `QQ`[i1] becomes m1 plus (`QQ`[i1] minus m1)·k. When ty is 1 (twist): w is `PF`[o+2]; i0, i1, m0, m1 and z0 are read as for the taper; t is (the negation of w)·(`QQ`[a] minus z0); cs is fs_cos(t); sn is fs_sin(t); u is `QQ`[i0] minus m0; v is `QQ`[i1] minus m1; `QQ`[i0] becomes (m0 plus cs·u) minus sn·v; `QQ`[i1] becomes (m1 plus sn·u) plus cs·v. When ty is 2 (bend): b is `PF`[o+2] as an int; th is `PF`[o+3], sg is `PF`[o+4], mb is `PF`[o+5], rb is `PF`[o+6], ct is `PF`[o+7], st is `PF`[o+8], z0 is `PF`[o+9], L is `PF`[o+10]; u is (`QQ`[b] minus mb)·sg; v is `QQ`[a] minus z0; ph is fs_atan2(v, rb minus u); when ph is at most 0 nothing changes; otherwise, when ph is at most th, U is rb minus hyp2(u minus rb, v) and VV is ph·rb, else rx is u minus rb, U is (rb plus rx·ct) minus v·st and VV is (L plus rx·st) plus v·ct; then `QQ`[b] becomes mb plus U·sg and `QQ`[a] becomes z0 plus VV.

Shape header. The header of shape s starts at h equal to `SHI`[8s+3] in `PF`: mirror signs at h, h+1, h+2; culling box minimum at h+3 to h+5 and maximum at h+6 to h+8; lc at h+9; blend k at h+10; color at h+11 to h+13; center c at h+14 to h+16; rotation R0 to R8 at h+17 to h+25; offset o at h+26; noise amp at h+27, nsz at h+28 and seed sd0, sd1, sd2 at h+29 to h+31; lip at h+32; onion at h+33; shell at h+34; then nd deformer ops of 12 reals each from h+35; then the copy transforms TF (12 reals each, nT of them) from tf equal to h plus 35 plus 12·nd; then the copy boxes BF (6 reals each) from bf equal to tf plus 12·nT. The flags bits are 1 (plain), 2 (rotated), 4 (noise), 8 (lip is not 1), 16 (onion), 32 (shell), 64 (own color).

`one(s int, x real, y real, z real) real`: h is `SHI`[8s+3] and fl is `SHI`[8s+2]; when fl and 1 is not 0 it returns prim(s, x, y, z). Otherwise c0, c1, c2 are `PF`[h+14], `PF`[h+15], `PF`[h+16]; X is x minus c0, Y is y minus c1, Z is z minus c2; when fl and 2 is not 0, with R0 to R8 equal to `PF`[h+17] to `PF`[h+25]: `QQ`[0] becomes ((c0 plus R0·X) plus R3·Y) plus R6·Z, `QQ`[1] becomes ((c1 plus R1·X) plus R4·Y) plus R7·Z and `QQ`[2] becomes ((c2 plus R2·X) plus R5·Y) plus R8·Z; otherwise `QQ`[0], `QQ`[1], `QQ`[2] become x, y, z. Then for j from `SHI`[8s+5] minus 1 down to 0: dinv(h plus 35 plus 12·j). d is prim(s, `QQ`[0], `QQ`[1], `QQ`[2]) minus `PF`[h+26]. When fl and 4 is not 0: nsz is `PF`[h+28] and d becomes d plus `PF`[h+27]·fbm(`QQ`[0] divided by nsz plus `PF`[h+29], `QQ`[1] divided by nsz plus `PF`[h+30], `QQ`[2] divided by nsz plus `PF`[h+31]). When fl and 8 is not 0, d becomes d divided by `PF`[h+32]. When fl and 16 is not 0, d becomes abs(d) minus `PF`[h+33]. When fl and 32 is not 0 it returns jmax(d, (the negation of d) minus `PF`[h+34]); otherwise it returns d.

`shp(s int, x real, y real, z real) real` is the distance of shape s with its mirror signs and copies: h is `SHI`[8s+3], nT is `SHI`[8s+4], nd is `SHI`[8s+5]; mx is x·`PF`[h], my is y·`PF`[h+1], mz is z·`PF`[h+2]. When nT is 1 it returns one(s, mx, my, mz). Otherwise tf is h plus 35 plus 12·nd, bf is tf plus 12·nT, lc is `PF`[h+9], and m starts at 1e9; for i from 0 below nT: b is bf plus 6·i; ex is jmax(jmax(`PF`[b] minus mx, 0.0), mx minus `PF`[b+3]); ey is jmax(jmax(`PF`[b+1] minus my, 0.0), my minus `PF`[b+4]); ez is jmax(jmax(`PF`[b+2] minus mz, 0.0), mz minus `PF`[b+5]); skip this copy when (ex is above 0 or ey is above 0 or ez is above 0) and len3(ex, ey, ez) divided by lc is at least m; t is tf plus 12·i; px is mx minus `PF`[t+9], py is my minus `PF`[t+10], pz is mz minus `PF`[t+11]; v is one(s, (`PF`[t]·px plus `PF`[t+3]·py) plus `PF`[t+6]·pz, (`PF`[t+1]·px plus `PF`[t+4]·py) plus `PF`[t+7]·pz, (`PF`[t+2]·px plus `PF`[t+5]·py) plus `PF`[t+8]·pz); when v is below m, m becomes v. It returns m.

Brushes. Brush b stores 8 ints at `BRI`[8b] to `BRI`[8b+7]: kind (0 inflate, 1 dent, 2 crease, 3 smooth, 4 flatten, 5 grab, 6 clay, 7 layer, 8 scrape, 9 pinch, 10 snake), ng (mirror sign triples), np (stroke points), o (its data offset in `PF`) and nps (snake path points). Its data at `PF`[o] on: r at o, s at o+1, box low at o+2 to o+4, box high at o+5 to o+7, centroid C at o+8 to o+10, normal N at o+11 to o+13, hh at o+14, be at o+15, grab strength g at o+16, grab offset at o+17 to o+19, then the ng sign triples from o+20, then the np stroke points from o+20 plus 3·ng, then the nps path points from o+20 plus 3·ng plus 3·np. Its state is 9 reals of `BS` from 9·b: the picked point Q at 9b to 9b+2, the picked signs G at 9b+3 to 9b+5, the nearest stroke point NP at 9b+6 to 9b+8.

`near(b int, x real, y real, z real, np bool) real`: o is `BRI`[8b+3]; n is `BRI`[8b+2]; pp is o plus 20 plus 3·`BRI`[8b+1]. When n is 1: when np, `BS`[9b+6], `BS`[9b+7], `BS`[9b+8] become `PF`[pp], `PF`[pp+1], `PF`[pp+2]; it returns len3(x minus `PF`[pp], y minus `PF`[pp+1], z minus `PF`[pp+2]). Otherwise m starts at 1e9; for i from 0 while i plus 1 is below n: d is segd(x, y, z, pp plus 3·i, pp plus 3·i plus 3) and t is `ST` right after it; when d is below m: m becomes d and, when np, for c from 0 below 3, `BS`[9b+6+c] becomes `PF`[pp+3i+c] plus (`PF`[pp+3i+3+c] minus `PF`[pp+3i+c])·t. It returns m.

`pick(b int, x real, y real, z real) real`: o is `BRI`[8b+3]; best starts at 1e18; for g from 0 below `BRI`[8b+1]: gx, gy, gz are `PF`[o+20+3g], `PF`[o+21+3g], `PF`[o+22+3g]; X is x·gx, Y is y·gy, Z is z·gz; skip when X is at most `PF`[o+2] or X is at least `PF`[o+5] or Y is at most `PF`[o+3] or Y is at least `PF`[o+6] or Z is at most `PF`[o+4] or Z is at least `PF`[o+7]; t is near(b, X, Y, Z, false); when t is below best: best becomes t, `BS`[9b], `BS`[9b+1], `BS`[9b+2] become X, Y, Z and `BS`[9b+3], `BS`[9b+4], `BS`[9b+5] become gx, gy, gz. It returns best divided by `PF`[o].

`wf(kind int, t real) real` is 0.0 when t is at least 1; otherwise fs_pow(1.0 minus t, 3.0) when kind is 2; otherwise, when kind is 7, 1.0 when t is below 0.5 and else 1.0 minus sm((t minus 0.5)·2.0); otherwise u·u with u equal to 1.0 minus t·t.

`warp(b int, x real, y real, z real)`: o is `BRI`[8b+3]; w is wf(5, pick(b, x, y, z))·`PF`[o+16]; when bad(w), `WX`, `WY`, `WZ` become x, y, z; otherwise `WX` becomes x minus (`PF`[o+17]·w)·`BS`[9b+3], `WY` becomes y minus (`PF`[o+18]·w)·`BS`[9b+4] and `WZ` becomes z minus (`PF`[o+19]·w)·`BS`[9b+5].

`base(x0 real, y0 real, z0 real, wc bool) real` blends all shapes: x, y, z start as x0, y0, z0; for i from `NW` minus 1 down to 0: warp(`LW`[i], x, y, z), then x, y, z become `WX`, `WY`, `WZ`. d starts at 1e9. When wc: `CO`[0], `CO`[1], `CO`[2] become `DC`[0], `DC`[1], `DC`[2] and `CO`[3] becomes 0.0. Then for s from 0 below `NS` in order: h is `SHI`[8s+3]; ex is jmax(jmax(`PF`[h+3] minus x, 0.0), x minus `PF`[h+6]); ey is jmax(jmax(`PF`[h+4] minus y, 0.0), y minus `PF`[h+7]); ez is jmax(jmax(`PF`[h+5] minus z, 0.0), z minus `PF`[h+8]); bd is len3(ex, ey, ez) divided by `PF`[h+9] when ex is above 0 or ey is above 0 or ez is above 0, otherwise 0.0; k is `PF`[h+10]; kb is k plus `CK`; kq is kb when wc, else k; op is `SHI`[8s+1]; skip this shape when op is 0 and bd minus kq is at least d, or when op is 1 and bd is at least kq minus d. b is shp(s, x, y, z). own is (`SHI`[8s+2] and 64) not 0; the color cc0, cc1, cc2 is `PF`[h+11], `PF`[h+12], `PF`[h+13] when own, else `DC`[0], `DC`[1], `DC`[2]; mi is `SHI`[8s+6] as a real.
When op is 0: when wc, hc is cl(0.5 plus (0.5·(b minus d)) divided by kb, 0.0, 1.0) when kb is above 0, otherwise 0.0 when b is below d and 1.0 when not; `CO`[j] becomes ccj plus (`CO`[j] minus ccj)·hc for j equal to 0, 1 and 2; and when b is below d, `CO`[3] becomes mi. Then when k is above 0: hk is cl(0.5 plus (0.5·(b minus d)) divided by k, 0.0, 1.0) and d becomes (b plus (d minus b)·hk) minus (k·hk)·(1.0 minus hk); otherwise when b is below d, d becomes b.
When op is 1: when wc and own, hc is cl(0.5 minus (0.5·(d plus b)) divided by kb, 0.0, 1.0) when kb is above 0, otherwise 1.0 when the negation of b is above d and 0.0 when not; `CO`[j] becomes `CO`[j] plus (ccj minus `CO`[j])·hc for j equal to 0, 1 and 2; and when the negation of b is above d, `CO`[3] becomes mi. Then when k is above 0: hk is cl(0.5 minus (0.5·(d plus b)) divided by k, 0.0, 1.0) and d becomes (d plus ((the negation of b) minus d)·hk) plus (k·hk)·(1.0 minus hk); otherwise when the negation of b is above d, d becomes the negation of b.
Otherwise (op 2): when k is above 0: hk is cl(0.5 minus (0.5·(b minus d)) divided by k, 0.0, 1.0) and d becomes (b plus (d minus b)·hk) plus (k·hk)·(1.0 minus hk); otherwise d becomes jmax(d, b).
It returns d.

`stg(lv int, x real, y real, z real, wc bool) real` is the distance after the first lv additive brushes. When lv is 0 it returns base(x, y, z, wc). Otherwise b is `LA`[lv minus 1], kind is `BRI`[8b], o is `BRI`[8b+3], r is `PF`[o], s is `PF`[o+1], and q is 9·b.
When kind is 9 or 10: t is pick(b, x, y, z); when t is at least 1 it returns stg(lv minus 1, x, y, z, wc). wm starts at 0.0. When kind is 9: near(b, `BS`[q], `BS`[q+1], `BS`[q+2], true) is called for its effect; w is wf(9, t); be is `PF`[o+15]; for c from 0 below 3, `BS`[q+c] becomes `BS`[q+c] plus ((`BS`[q+c] minus `BS`[q+6+c])·be)·w; wm becomes (2.5·be)·w. When kind is 10: n is `BRI`[8b+4]; ps is o plus 20 plus 3·`BRI`[8b+1] plus 3·`BRI`[8b+2]; for i from n minus 2 down to 0: e is ps plus 3·(i plus 1); f is ps plus 3·i; ri is r·(1.0 minus ((s·0.7)·((i plus 1) as a real)) divided by ((n minus 1) as a real)); tt is len3(`BS`[q] minus `PF`[e], `BS`[q+1] minus `PF`[e+1], `BS`[q+2] minus `PF`[e+2]) divided by ri; w is u·u with u equal to 1.0 minus tt·tt when tt is below 1, else 0.0; when bad(w) skip to the next i; for c from 0 below 3, `BS`[q+c] becomes `BS`[q+c] minus (`PF`[e+c] minus `PF`[f+c])·w; wm becomes jmax(wm, 0.6·w). Either way it then returns stg(lv minus 1, `BS`[q]·`BS`[q+3], `BS`[q+1]·`BS`[q+4], `BS`[q+2]·`BS`[q+5], wc) divided by (1.0 plus wm).
Every other kind: d is stg(lv minus 1, x, y, z, wc), and only after that w is wf(kind, pick(b, x, y, z)); when bad(w) it returns d. pl, used only by kinds 4, 6 and 8 and read after pick, is ((`BS`[q] minus `PF`[o+8])·`PF`[o+11] plus (`BS`[q+1] minus `PF`[o+9])·`PF`[o+12]) plus (`BS`[q+2] minus `PF`[o+10])·`PF`[o+13]. Kind 0 returns d minus ((s·r)·0.5)·w; kind 1 returns d plus ((s·r)·0.5)·w; kind 2 returns d plus ((s·r)·0.45)·w; kind 7 returns d minus ((s·r)·0.3)·w; kind 4 returns d plus ((pl minus d)·s)·w; kind 6 returns d plus (jmin(d, pl minus (s·r)·0.3) minus d)·w; kind 8 returns d plus (jmax(d, pl plus (s·r)·0.15) minus d)·w; any other kind (smooth): hh is `PF`[o+14]; a1 is stg(lv minus 1, x plus hh, y, z, false), a2 is stg(lv minus 1, x minus hh, y, z, false), a3 is stg(lv minus 1, x, y plus hh, z, false), a4 is stg(lv minus 1, x, y minus hh, z, false), a5 is stg(lv minus 1, x, y, z plus hh, false), a6 is stg(lv minus 1, x, y, z minus hh, false), called in that order; a is ((((a1 plus a2) plus a3) plus a4) plus a5) plus a6, divided by 6.0; it returns d plus ((a minus d)·s)·w.

`dval(x real, y real, z real) real` is stg(`NA`, x, y, z, false).

The exported function `fs_load(ns i32, nb i32, nw i32, na i32, nf i32)` stores `NS`, `NB`, `NW` and `NA`; each of these arrays is emptied and then gets `reserve`(its size), then is resized to its size in zeros: `SHI` (8·ns), `BRI` (8·nb), `LW` (nw), `LA` (na), `PF` (nf), `BS` (9·nb), `DC` (3), `CO` (4), `QQ` (3), `PR` (12) and `TOP` (4). It reports `fs_view_i32(1, SHI)`, `fs_view_i32(2, BRI)`, `fs_view_i32(3, LW)`, `fs_view_i32(4, LA)`, `fs_view_f64(5, PF)`, `fs_view_f64(6, BS)`, `fs_view_f64(7, DC)`, `fs_view_f64(8, CO)` and `fs_view_f64(9, PR)`. The exported function `fs_ck(v real)` sets `CK` to v. The exported function `fs_d(x real, y real, z real) real` returns dval(x, y, z). The exported function `fs_c(x real, y real, z real)` calls stg(`NA`, x, y, z, true) and drops its result (the color is left in `CO`). The exported function `fs_f(i i32, x real, y real, z real) real` returns shp(i, x, y, z).

`sample(nx int, ny int, nz int, l0 real, l1 real, l2 real, h real, thr real)` fills `V` block by block. Bit q of `DN` is (`DN`[q shifted right 3] shifted right (q and 7)) and 1; setting bit q ors `DN`[q shifted right 3] with 1 shifted left (q and 7). For bk from 0 below nz in steps of 4, inside it bj from 0 below ny in steps of 4, inside it bi from 0 below nx in steps of 4: ei is the smaller of bi plus 4 and nx minus 1, ej is the smaller of bj plus 4 and ny minus 1, ek is the smaller of bk plus 4 and nz minus 1; dc is dval(l0 plus (((bi plus ei) as a real) divided by 2.0)·h, l1 plus (((bj plus ej) as a real) divided by 2.0)·h, l2 plus (((bk plus ek) as a real) divided by 2.0)·h); `EVC` grows by 1; mb is the largest of ei minus bi, ej minus bj and ek minus bk; far is true when abs(dc) is above thr·(the larger of 1.0 and (mb as a real) divided by 4.0). Then for k from bk to ek inclusive, inside it j from bj to ej inclusive, inside it i from bi to ei inclusive: q is i plus nx·(j plus ny·k); when bit q of `DN` is set, skip; when far, `V`[q] becomes dc as f32 and skip; otherwise `V`[q] becomes dval(l0 plus (i as a real)·h, l1 plus (j as a real)·h, l2 plus (k as a real)·h) as f32, bit q of `DN` is set, and `EVC` grows by 1.

`cells(k int, nx int, ny int, l0 real, l1 real, l2 real, h real)` makes the surface-nets vertices of cell layer k (`CV8` already holds 8 entries, sized by fs_mesh; cells never resizes it). sw is (nx minus 1)·(ny minus 1); sb is (k mod 2)·sw; the sw entries of `CV` from sb are set to −1. For j from 0 below ny minus 1, inside it i from 0 below nx minus 1: neg starts at 0; for c from 0 below 8: `CV8`[c] becomes `V`[(i plus (c and 1)) plus nx·((j plus ((c shifted right 1) and 1)) plus ny·(k plus (c shifted right 2)))] as a real, and neg grows by 1 when `CV8`[c] is below 0. When neg is 0 or 8, skip this cell. sx, sy, sz start at 0.0 and m at 0; for e from 0 below 12: a is `EDG`[2e], b is `EDG`[2e+1]; va is `CV8`[a], vb is `CV8`[b]; when (va below 0) equals (vb below 0), skip this edge; t is va divided by (va minus vb); sx becomes sx plus (((a and 1) as a real) plus (((b and 1) minus (a and 1)) as a real)·t); sy becomes sy plus ((((a shifted right 1) and 1) as a real) plus ((((b shifted right 1) and 1) minus ((a shifted right 1) and 1)) as a real)·t); sz becomes sz plus (((a shifted right 2) as a real) plus (((b shifted right 2) minus (a shifted right 2)) as a real)·t); m grows by 1. `CV`[sb plus i plus (nx minus 1)·j] becomes `NVX` as i32; then puf(`POS`, l0 plus ((i as a real) plus sx divided by (m as a real))·h), puf(`POS`, l1 plus ((j as a real) plus sy divided by (m as a real))·h), puf(`POS`, l2 plus ((k as a real) plus sz divided by (m as a real))·h), and `NVX` grows by 1.

`quad(a int, b int, c int, d int, flip bool)`: when any of a, b, c and d is below 0 nothing happens; otherwise when flip it does pui(`IDX`, a), pui(`IDX`, c), pui(`IDX`, b), pui(`IDX`, a), pui(`IDX`, d), pui(`IDX`, c); otherwise pui(`IDX`, a), pui(`IDX`, b), pui(`IDX`, c), pui(`IDX`, a), pui(`IDX`, c), pui(`IDX`, d). `cell(i int, j int, k int, nx int, ny int) int` is `CV`[(k mod 2)·(nx minus 1)·(ny minus 1) plus i plus (nx minus 1)·j] as an int.

`quads(k int, nx int, ny int, nz int)` makes the faces of grid layer k. For j from 0 below ny, inside it i from 0 below nx: v0 is `V`[i plus nx·(j plus ny·k)] as a real; s0 is v0 below 0; fl is v0 at least 0; ini is i above 0 and i below nx minus 1; inj is j above 0 and j below ny minus 1; ink is k above 0 and k below nz minus 1. First, when i is below nx minus 1 and inj and ink and s0 differs from (`V`[(i plus 1) plus nx·(j plus ny·k)] below 0): quad(cell(i, j−1, k−1), cell(i, j, k−1), cell(i, j, k), cell(i, j−1, k), fl). Second, when j is below ny minus 1 and ini and ink and s0 differs from (`V`[i plus nx·((j plus 1) plus ny·k)] below 0): quad(cell(i−1, j, k−1), cell(i−1, j, k), cell(i, j, k), cell(i, j, k−1), fl). Third, when k is below nz minus 1 and ini and inj and s0 differs from (`V`[i plus nx·(j plus ny·(k plus 1))] below 0): quad(cell(i−1, j−1, k), cell(i, j−1, k), cell(i, j, k), cell(i−1, j, k), fl). (Every cell call also passes nx and ny.)

`shade(h real)` sets normals, projects the vertices onto the surface and colors them. nv is `NVX`; `NRM` and `COL` are emptied and then get `reserve`(3·nv), then are resized to 3·nv zeros; `MAT` is emptied and then gets `reserve`(nv), then is resized to nv zeros; e is h·0.5. For v from 0 below nv: x, y, z are `POS`[3v], `POS`[3v+1], `POS`[3v+2] as reals; gx is dval(x plus e, y, z) minus dval(x minus e, y, z); then gy is dval(x, y plus e, z) minus dval(x, y minus e, z); then gz is dval(x, y, z plus e) minus dval(x, y, z minus e) (the six calls in exactly this order); l is len3(gx, gy, gz), replaced by 1.0 when bad(l); `NRM`[3v] becomes (gx divided by l) as f32, `NRM`[3v+1] becomes (gy divided by l) as f32, `NRM`[3v+2] becomes (gz divided by l) as f32; then kk is cl((((the negation of dval(x, y, z))·2.0)·e) divided by l, the negation of h, h); X is x plus (gx divided by l)·kk, Y is y plus (gy divided by l)·kk, Z is z plus (gz divided by l)·kk; `POS`[3v], `POS`[3v+1], `POS`[3v+2] become X, Y, Z as f32; then stg(`NA`, X, Y, Z, true) is called for its colour; `COL`[3v], `COL`[3v+1], `COL`[3v+2] become `CO`[0], `CO`[1], `CO`[2] as f32 and `MAT`[v] becomes `CO`[3] as an int as u8.

`props()` measures the mesh into `PR`. vol and area start at 0.0; for t from 0 below the length of `IDX` in steps of 3: a is 3·`IDX`[t], b is 3·`IDX`[t+1], c is 3·`IDX`[t+2] (ints); ax, ay, az are `POS`[a], `POS`[a+1], `POS`[a+2] as reals; bx, by, bz are `POS`[b], `POS`[b+1], `POS`[b+2] as reals; cx, cy, cz are `POS`[c], `POS`[c+1], `POS`[c+2] as reals; vol becomes vol plus (((ax·(by·cz minus bz·cy) minus ay·(bx·cz minus bz·cx)) plus az·(bx·cy minus by·cx)) divided by 6.0); ux is bx minus ax, uy is by minus ay, uz is bz minus az, wx is cx minus ax, wy is cy minus ay, wz is cz minus az; area becomes area plus len3(uy·wz minus uz·wy, uz·wx minus ux·wz, ux·wy minus uy·wx) divided by 2.0. Then mn is a three-entry local list of 1e9 and mx a three-entry local list of −1e9; for v from 0 below `NVX`, inside it c from 0 below 3: q is `POS`[3v+c] as a real; when q is below mn[c], mn[c] becomes q; separately, when q is above mx[c], mx[c] becomes q. `PR`[0] becomes vol, `PR`[1] becomes area, `PR`[2], `PR`[3], `PR`[4] become mn[0], mn[1], mn[2], `PR`[5], `PR`[6], `PR`[7] become mx[0], mx[1], mx[2], `PR`[8] becomes `EVC` as a real, `PR`[9] becomes `NVX` as a real and `PR`[10] becomes the length of `IDX` as a real.

The exported function `fs_mesh(nx i32, ny i32, nz i32, l0 real, l1 real, l2 real, h real, thr real, ck2 real)` meshes the loaded program on the grid of nx·ny·nz points with corner (l0, l1, l2) and spacing h (ints from here on). n is nx·ny·nz. `V` is emptied and then gets `reserve`(n), then is resized to n zeros; `DN` is emptied and then gets `reserve`(n divided by 8 plus 1), then is resized to n divided by 8 plus 1 zeros; `CV` is emptied and then gets `reserve`(2·(nx minus 1)·(ny minus 1)), then is resized to that many zeros; `CV8` is emptied and then gets `reserve`(8), then is resized to 8 zeros; `POS` and `IDX` are emptied; `NVX` and `EVC` become 0. Then sample(nx, ny, nz, l0, l1, l2, h, thr). Then for k from 0 below nz: when k is below nz minus 1, cells(k, nx, ny, l0, l1, l2, h); then quads(k, nx, ny, nz). Then `CK` becomes ck2, then shade(h), then props(). Finally it reports `fs_view_f32(10, POS)`, `fs_view_i32(11, IDX)`, `fs_view_f32(12, NRM)`, `fs_view_f32(13, COL)` and `fs_view_u8(14, MAT)`.

The exported function `fs_skin_load(np i32, nb i32, nv i32)` sizes the skin arrays; each is emptied and then gets `reserve`(its size), then is resized to its size in zeros: `KP` (3·np), `KK` (np), `DP` (np), `AC` (nb), `JN` (4·nv) and `WT` (4·nv). It reports `fs_view_i32(15, KP)` and `fs_view_f64(16, KK)`. Part p is shape `KP`[3p] with bone `KP`[3p+1], rigid when `KP`[3p+2] is not 0, and blend `KK`[p].

The exported function `fs_skin(np i32, nb i32, nv i32, size real)` computes the skin weights of the nv vertices in `POS`. For v from 0 below nv: x, y, z are `POS`[3v], `POS`[3v+1], `POS`[3v+2] as reals; dmin starts at 1e18, pm at 0 and soft at 1e18; for p from 0 below np: `DP`[p] becomes jmax(shp(`KP`[3p], x, y, z), 0.0); when `DP`[p] is below dmin, dmin becomes `DP`[p] and pm becomes p; when `KP`[3p+2] is 0 and `DP`[p] is below soft, soft becomes `DP`[p]. When np is 0 or `KP`[3pm+2] is not 0: `JN`[4v] becomes `KP`[3pm+1] when np is above 0 and 0 otherwise, `WT`[4v] becomes 1.0 as f32, and the loop goes on to the next vertex. Otherwise every entry of `AC` becomes 0.0; for p from 0 below np, skipping p when `KP`[3p+2] is not 0: sg is jmax(`KK`[p]·0.6, size·0.012); bo is `KP`[3p+1]; when bo is at least 0 and below nb, `AC`[bo] becomes `AC`[bo] plus fs_exp((the negation of (`DP`[p] minus soft)) divided by sg). Then cnt is the smaller of 4 and nb; for c from 0 below cnt: best starts at −1; for i from 0 below nb, skipping i when it equals any of `TOP`[0] to `TOP`[c−1]: when best is −1 or `AC`[i] is above `AC`[best], best becomes i; then `TOP`[c] becomes best. sum starts at 0.0 and for c from 0 below cnt becomes sum plus `AC`[`TOP`[c]]. For c from 0 below cnt: `JN`[4v+c] becomes `TOP`[c]; `WT`[4v+c] becomes (`AC`[`TOP`[c]] divided by sum when not bad(sum), otherwise 1.0 when c is 0 and 0.0 when not) as f32. At the end it reports `fs_view_i32(17, JN)` and `fs_view_f32(18, WT)`.

`main()` does nothing.
